WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.716
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.745 --> 00:00:13.543
My name is John Doe at Kleiner Perkins
said, one time he said,

00:00:13.543 --> 00:00:18.149
if you're trying to figure out if
you're an entrepreneur, you're not,

00:00:18.149 --> 00:00:21.165
which I always thought
was kind of interesting.

00:00:21.165 --> 00:00:24.134
If you need structure,
then nothing wrong with that.

00:00:24.134 --> 00:00:27.800
Big companies need a lot of people,
they're good places to work.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.572
And if that's you, that's fine, go big.

00:00:33.572 --> 00:00:34.787
Lots of big companies recruiting here.

00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:38.504
Lots of big companies are great
places to work for entire careers.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:42.533
Career transitions that I've watched,
are historically quite easy,

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:47.105
from large companies to small ones, and
quite hard from small ones to large ones.

00:00:47.105 --> 00:00:51.711
And so if you are going to go from
a big company background into

00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:55.189
the entrepreneurial world, think long and

00:00:55.189 --> 00:01:01.210
hard about that because it won't be that
easy to go back where you came from.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.201
Maybe if it's a year or two,
and you duck back quick or

00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:10.102
you go back to the company you work for,
you might be able to do it.

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:13.101
But then I would almost say you weren't
really all that committed to going into

00:01:13.101 --> 00:01:14.562
the startup world in the first place.

00:01:14.562 --> 00:01:17.164
If you were looking for
an opportunity to go back.

00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:21.407
Like I said, if it scares you, go big.

00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:25.678
The failures, particularly in

00:01:25.678 --> 00:01:30.899
the entrepreneurial community here,

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:35.022
are really almost no big deal.

00:01:35.022 --> 00:01:39.702
But they tend to be perceived as a much
bigger deal by the bigger companies.

00:01:39.702 --> 00:01:42.290
The bigger, more mature companies,
and it's just a fact of life.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:47.666
I think many of you have
probably experienced that.

00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:51.868
So I think the likelihood is, if you
are committing to an entrepreneurial

00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:54.938
career path,
that's a commitment you need to make.

00:01:54.938 --> 00:01:59.213
And you need to probably believe
that it's a career long commitment,

00:01:59.213 --> 00:02:03.137
that that's where you're going to
spend the rest of your career.

00:02:03.137 --> 00:02:06.364
Can you handle the risk?

00:02:06.364 --> 00:02:09.264
What do you do if it fails?

00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:14.633
Some cultures, some geographies,
failure is really hard.

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:17.550
It's really hard to explain or
somebody said once,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.548
how are you going to go home and
tell your mother that your company failed?

00:02:21.548 --> 00:02:23.714
He grew up around Silicon Valley,
was like, okay, big deal.

00:02:23.714 --> 00:02:25.043
What are you going to do next?

00:02:25.043 --> 00:02:27.516
But there's some places in the country and

00:02:27.516 --> 00:02:30.752
in the world where that's
going to be really hard for you.

00:02:30.752 --> 00:02:34.262
And if it is, and
you don't feel like you're up for it,

00:02:34.262 --> 00:02:36.820
maybe that's not where you ought to go.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.781
Are the leaders of the startup
professionals or are they friends?

00:02:40.781 --> 00:02:45.383
I put up a big red flag every time I see a
startup that's three college roommates to

00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:49.648
get together because they want to start
a company, and I'll be the CEO and so

00:02:49.648 --> 00:02:52.717
you'd be the marketing VP,
and what do you want to be?

00:02:52.717 --> 00:02:53.960
Okay, engineering.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.537
Wow, we got to have one.

00:02:55.537 --> 00:02:57.200
Okay, well, you're really not very good.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.660
That's a bad way to get a company started.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.412
And if you get into a company where
the officers are friends, and

00:03:04.412 --> 00:03:10.309
they wouldn't be picked for their talent
in a fair fight against other people,

00:03:10.309 --> 00:03:14.006
this has a very high
probability of coming out with

00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:17.549
the worst possible outcome
you could imagine.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:21.447
Which is you lose the company,
you lose the job, and

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:24.997
you probably lose your
friends in the process.

00:03:24.997 --> 00:03:29.612
So I just say, massive caveats on
going in any startup where a bunch

00:03:29.612 --> 00:03:33.087
of your friends get together and
choose upsides.

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:36.656
Is the financing solid for
at least a year?

00:03:36.656 --> 00:03:40.504
Because you don't want to
be coming into something,

00:03:40.504 --> 00:03:45.314
unless there's at least a year of
runway there before the company

00:03:45.314 --> 00:03:50.397
needs to raise money,
because maybe it won't, and that's tough.

00:03:50.397 --> 00:03:54.218
As I said, if it's a tough choice, say no.

00:03:54.218 --> 00:03:58.211
If it's a startup, you want to be
focused on a couple of things.

00:03:58.211 --> 00:04:00.636
And I say, will you be at the table?

00:04:00.636 --> 00:04:04.172
At the table means,
are you going to be in the group,

00:04:04.172 --> 00:04:08.143
the small group of people
that makes the key decisions?

00:04:08.143 --> 00:04:12.202
They're in a closed room with the door
closed when something big is coming down,

00:04:12.202 --> 00:04:15.559
and bet the company or really
important decisions have to be made.

00:04:15.559 --> 00:04:19.867
If you're at that table, doesn't matter
a whole lot what your job title is,

00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:24.260
what your compensation is really, or any
else because it'll all be taken care of.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:29.307
If you're not at the table, and you're
outside looking through the keyhole trying

00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:34.151
to figure out what's going on in that room
when the key decisions are being made.

00:04:34.151 --> 00:04:37.415
I would say to you,
if it's a start up, most of the time,

00:04:37.415 --> 00:04:39.870
the risk reward ratio for
you is not right.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.559
That puts you in the world
of four box matrixes,

00:04:43.559 --> 00:04:48.264
in the high risk, low reward box,
and I don't think that's

00:04:48.264 --> 00:04:52.987
a box that you ought to be
intentionally aiming yourself to.

00:04:52.987 --> 00:04:58.072
That if the company is wildly successful,
you make a little bit of money,

00:04:58.072 --> 00:05:01.857
and you weren't really part
of the team that drove it.

00:05:01.857 --> 00:05:05.725
On the other hand,
if you're in the high risk,

00:05:05.725 --> 00:05:09.323
high reward box, yeah, that can be great.

00:05:09.323 --> 00:05:10.562
That's a mature startup.

00:05:10.562 --> 00:05:14.725
That's not a pure startup from zero.

00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:20.034
If they've done a series B, and
they've got financing for a year or more,

00:05:20.034 --> 00:05:25.765
and they're growing rapidly, then you
might relax that because they're going to

00:05:25.765 --> 00:05:31.520
be hiring a lot of people, and they're not
all going to have a seat at the key table.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:38.879
Then the question though to ask is, will
I have a seat at some interesting table?

00:05:38.879 --> 00:05:43.286
And does the person that
I'm going to be working for

00:05:43.286 --> 00:05:46.415
maybe have a seat at the big table?

00:05:46.415 --> 00:05:47.180
[MUSIC]

